<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Adil Shahi dynasty</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1490">1490</a>–<a href="/years/1686">1686</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Bijapur Sultanate</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Adil Shahi dynasty ruled the Bijapur Sultanate in the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a> from 1490, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yusuf-adil-shah">Yusuf Adil Shah</a> broke away from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahmani-sultanate">Bahmani Sultanate</a>, until the Mughal emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bijapur">Bijapur</a> in 1686. Its sultans built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gol-gumbaz">Gol Gumbaz</a> and other monuments. Many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lingayatism">Lingayat</a> families, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhonsles">Bhonsles</a>, served it as commanders and revenue holders before founding their own states.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/adil-shahi-dynasty.jpg" alt="Adil Shahi dynasty" width="320" height="296" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Map created from DEMIS Mapserver, which are public domain. Koba-chan. Reference: [1] Borders created by Noorullah21 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Adil_Shahis.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The origin of the founder, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yusuf-adil-shah">Yusuf Adil Shah</a>, is uncertain. He may have been a Georgian slave bought by the Bahmani statesman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-gawan">Mahmud Gawan</a>, while other historians call him Persian or Turkoman. The historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/firishta">Firishta</a> made him a son of the Ottoman sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murad-ii">Murad II</a>, which modern historians dispute. Under the Bahmani sultan Muhammad Shah III he became governor of the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bijapur">Bijapur</a>, and in 1490 he made it a de facto independent state, formally independent with the Bahmani collapse in 1518. In 1503 he proclaimed Shi'a Islam the official religion, following Isma'il I of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavids</a>. Bijapur remained the capital throughout.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The sultanate fought constantly with its neighbours. The Portuguese took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goa">Goa</a> in 1510, the year Yusuf died, and Vijayanagara's ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishnadevaraya">Krishnadevaraya</a> defeated his son Isma'il at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raichur">Raichur</a> in 1520. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-adil-shah-i">Ali Adil Shah I</a>, who restored Shi'a Islam in 1558, joined the sultans of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golconda-fort">Golconda</a>, Ahmednagar and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bidar">Bidar</a> to defeat Vijayanagara at Talikota in 1565, after which Vijayanagara was sacked. By 1576 his conquests had doubled the sultanate's holdings. His nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibrahim-adil-shah-ii">Ibrahim Adil Shah II</a> came to the throne aged nine in 1580 and presided over prosperity and patronage, with population estimates for the capital as high as one million.</p><p>The sultanate reached its peak under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-adil-shah">Muhammad Adil Shah</a>, who ruled from 1627. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shah-jahan">Shah Jahan</a>'s failed siege of 1631 a treaty of 1636 made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bijapur">Bijapur</a> tributary to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a>, and its borders then reached from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabian-sea">Arabian Sea</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-bengal">Bay of Bengal</a>. Strained relations with nobles and the revolt of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shivaji">Shivaji</a> weakened it. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a> invaded in 1657, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-adil-shah-ii">Ali Adil Shah II</a> lost ground to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Marathas</a>, and the last sultan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikandar-adil-shah">Sikandar Adil Shah</a>, was four years old when he took the throne. A Mughal siege begun in April 1685 ended on 12 September 1686, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikandar-lodi">Sikandar</a> was taken captive.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Adil Shahi architecture is a Deccani variant of Indo-Islamic building, marked by large domes, turrets and calligraphic designs. The great Jami Masjid was commissioned in 1576 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibrahim-rauza">Ibrahim Rauza</a> was completed in 1626. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-adil-shah">Muhammad Adil Shah</a>'s mausoleum, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gol-gumbaz">Gol Gumbaz</a>, had a dome that was the largest in the Islamic world when it was nearly finished in 1656.</p><p>The sultans also encouraged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-miniature">miniature painting</a>, which spread widely under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-adil-shah-i">Ali Adil Shah I</a>, and writing in the Deccani language. Ibrahim II wrote the Kitab-i Nauras, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/firishta">Firishta</a> composed his history of the medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a>, the Tarikh-i Firishta, in his service.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bijapur Sultanate” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Around 1490 three of them effectively declared independence: Yusuf Adil Khan at Bijapur, Ahmad Nizam ul-Mulk at Ahmadnagar and Fathullah Imad ul-Mulk in Berar, founding the <b>Adil Shahi</b>, Nizam Shahi and Imad Shahi dynasties.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bahmani-sultanate">Bahmani Sultanate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Bijapur Sultanate</b> was one of five states that emerged from the break-up of the Bahmani Sultanate, which had ruled the Deccan from Gulbarga and later Bidar since 1347.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bijapur-sultanate">Bijapur Sultanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>His successors expanded the kingdom across the southern plateau, absorbing neighbouring chiefs and resisting the <b>Bijapur Sultanate</b>, Madurai and other powers.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-mysore-1399-1799">Kingdom of Mysore (1399-1799) · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Jath was founded in 1686 when the Chavan family, later known as Daphle, received the deshmukhi of the Jath area from the <b>Adil Shahi</b> sultans of Bijapur.</blockquote><a href="/state/jath-state">Jath State</a></li><li><blockquote>Kittur was founded in the late 16th century by a family of Lingayat chiefs who served the <b>Adil Shahi</b> sultans of Bijapur and held the title Desai.</blockquote><a href="/state/kittur-state">Kittur State</a></li><li><blockquote>The Sawant family, Bhonsle Marathas who had served the <b>Adil Shahi</b> sultans of Bijapur, established themselves as rulers of the Konkan country around Sawantwadi in 1627.</blockquote><a href="/state/savantvadi-state">Savantvadi State</a></li></ul></section>
